WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.181
In this video, we'll work through loop control, how you tell an agent when to stop, when to try

2
00:00:06.181 --> 00:00:09.694
again, and when to hand the problem to something else.

3
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:16.699
We'll cover termination conditions, turn budgets, infinite loop risk, and the distinction between

4
00:00:16.699 --> 00:00:18.488
iteration and escalation.

5
00:00:18.559 --> 00:00:26.159
These are reliability concerns, just as much as performance concerns, and every production agent needs them.

6
00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:32.639
An agent that never stops isn't autonomous, it's just expensive. So let's name the loop we're

7
00:00:32.639 --> 00:00:38.479
trying to control. You've already walked through perception, reasoning, action, and observation.

8
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:45.279
Anthropic's own framing compresses that same cycle into three blended phases. Gathering

9
00:00:45.279 --> 00:00:51.439
context covers perception and reasoning together, reading files, searching, or reviewing prior

10
00:00:51.439 --> 00:00:58.400
results before deciding the next move. Taking action is the tool call itself, querying a system

11
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:04.319
or changing state. Verifying work is the observation step, checking the result against the goal.

12
00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:11.760
Same loop, described at a coarser grain, so treat them as one cycle rather than rival answers.

13
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:17.760
It repeats until the job is done, and loop control is the discipline of deciding when it should stop.

14
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:25.040
Loop control has its own vocabulary, and precision here matters. A termination condition is the

15
00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:31.760
explicit criterion that causes the loop to stop. Without one, the loop has no reason to end.

16
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:37.040
A turn budget is a hard limit, set in the orchestration layer on how many iterations the

17
00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:43.760
agent is allowed to run. It's a programmatic guard, not a hint. An escalation is the controlled handoff

18
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.519
from the agent to a human operator or fallback system when the agent can't make further progress

19
00:01:49.519 --> 00:01:55.120
on its own. These three concepts work together. The termination condition tells the agent when

20
00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:01.040
it's done. The turn budget tells the runtime when to force a stop, and escalation is what

21
00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:07.519
happens when neither of those is enough to resolve the situation cleanly. There are four main reasons

22
00:02:07.519 --> 00:02:14.160
an agentic loop should stop. First, task complete. The agent has produced a final output, and there's

23
00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:20.880
nothing left to do. This is the happy path. Second, error threshold reached. If the agent has hit too

24
00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.960
many consecutive failures, continuing rarely helps and usually makes things worse. Third, budget

25
00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:32.800
exhausted. The turn limit or token budget set by the orchestrator has been reached. The loop halts

26
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:39.520
programmatically regardless of task state. Fourth, stop signal. The orchestrator or user explicitly

27
00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:45.199
tells the agent to stop. All four of these have to be handled. An agent that only checks for task

28
00:02:45.199 --> 00:02:51.360
completion will keep running through the other three without ever stopping. Infinite loops are

29
00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:57.039
one of the most common failure modes in agentic systems. They happen in a few ways. The simplest

30
00:02:57.119 --> 00:03:03.119
is a missing stop condition. The agent never decides the task is complete, so it keeps going.

31
00:03:03.119 --> 00:03:09.919
Another is always failing tools. A broken API call that returns an error every time, retried without

32
00:03:09.919 --> 00:03:15.520
limit. The most subtle is circular reasoning, where the model revisits the same decision point

33
00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:21.839
iteration after iteration without new information to break the cycle. None of these are exotic edge

34
00:03:21.839 --> 00:03:27.600
cases. They're predictable failure modes that show up during normal development. The fix in every

35
00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:34.639
case is the same. Explicit termination conditions and hard caps on iterations, baked in before the

36
00:03:34.639 --> 00:03:41.119
agent runs, not added after something goes wrong. Turn budgets are the primary programmatic defense

37
00:03:41.119 --> 00:03:46.559
against runaway loops, and there's a key design point here. They belong in the runtime layer,

38
00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:52.240
not in the model. You shouldn't rely on the model to count its own iterations and decide to stop.

39
00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:58.160
The orchestrator tracks iteration count. It enforces the limit. When the budget is exhausted,

40
00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:03.440
the runtime halts execution and surfaces whatever progress was made up to that point.

41
00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:09.759
Setting a reasonable max iterations limit before the agent starts is standard practice. What's

42
00:04:09.759 --> 00:04:15.199
reasonable depends on the task. A simple lookup might need three to five iterations. A research

43
00:04:15.199 --> 00:04:21.519
task might need 20. The point is that the number is chosen deliberately, not left open-ended. Bounded

44
00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:28.320
loops are auditable. Unbounded loops are liabilities. Progress versus spinning is a distinction worth

45
00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:34.559
spending time on, because spinning looks like activity without actually being it. An agent

46
00:04:34.559 --> 00:04:40.000
making progress produces a new observation each iteration that advances toward the goal.

47
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:46.000
Each tool call returns different data. The scope of the problem narrows. Something gets done. A

48
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:52.000
spinning agent, on the other hand, repeats similar actions without new results. Tool calls come back

49
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.760
with the same errors. The plan doesn't change. The context window grows, but the gap between the

50
00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:04.160
current state and the goal state doesn't shrink. The agent is generating tokens and consuming API

51
00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:10.079
calls without actually moving forward. Recognizing this pattern is the first step to stopping it.

52
00:05:11.279 --> 00:05:15.279
Detecting spinning in practice requires deliberate instrumentation.

53
00:05:15.279 --> 00:05:20.720
The simplest check is to compare successive observations. If two consecutive tool calls

54
00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:26.959
return nearly identical results, that's a signal. Tracking repeated tool calls with identical

55
00:05:26.959 --> 00:05:32.239
parameters is another approach. If the agent is calling the same search query three times in a

56
00:05:32.239 --> 00:05:38.399
row, something is wrong. You can also monitor the gap between goal state and current state across

57
00:05:38.399 --> 00:05:45.359
turns. If that gap isn't closing after several iterations, the agent is likely stuck. None of

58
00:05:45.359 --> 00:05:51.920
this detection has to be complex. Even a simple counter that flags when the last n observations

59
00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:57.679
were identical is enough to catch most spinning cases before they run up your API bill.

60
00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:04.720
When the agent hits a problem, it can iterate with new information, or it can escalate to a human or

61
00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:11.119
fallback system. Iteration makes sense when there's new data to work with, a different approach to try,

62
00:06:11.119 --> 00:06:17.519
or when the error is recoverable. Maybe the first search query was too broad. A more targeted query

63
00:06:17.519 --> 00:06:22.720
might get better results. Escalation makes sense when the agent has reached its authorization

64
00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:28.320
boundary, when it lacks the information it needs to proceed, or when a human judgment call is

65
00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:34.239
required. The important thing is that the boundary between iterate and escalate is explicit in the

66
00:06:34.239 --> 00:06:41.359
agent's design. Escalation isn't failure. It's a designed safety valve. An agent that escalates

67
00:06:41.359 --> 00:06:46.480
gracefully is more trustworthy than one that keeps cycling and hoping things improve.

68
00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:53.839
Loop control is a reliability concern, and it helps to understand exactly why. Runaway agents

69
00:06:53.839 --> 00:07:00.480
can exhaust API quotas and generate unexpected costs. Agents that don't escalate when they should

70
00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:06.399
can make unrecoverable decisions, taking actions the user would have stopped if they'd known. And

71
00:07:06.399 --> 00:07:13.519
agents without defined exit conditions are hard to reason about, audit, or debug. Proper termination

72
00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:19.760
conditions make agent behavior predictable. When something goes wrong, you can look at the logs and

73
00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:26.160
understand exactly why the agent stopped, what it was trying to do, and where it got stuck. That

74
00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:32.079
auditability is what makes it possible to improve the system over time. Reliability isn't just about

75
00:07:32.079 --> 00:07:38.079
what happens when things go right. Loop control is how you decide when an agent stops trying.

76
00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:43.839
It matters because a model with no exit condition will happily burn your entire budget,

77
00:07:43.839 --> 00:07:50.399
confirming the same failure over and over again. One thing to watch is the gap between an agent

78
00:07:50.399 --> 00:07:56.160
that's iterating and an agent that's just spinning. From the outside, those can look identical,

79
00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:01.920
and only the runtime can tell them apart. That's it for this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

